      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:12 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Scott G. Bullock (Petitioners)",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BULLOCK: Justice O'Connor, and may it please the Court: This case is about whether there are any limits on government's eminent domain power under the public use requirement of the Fifth Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Scott G. Bullock (Petitioners)",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BULLOCK: That is correct, Your Honor. We do not --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus briefs",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0000-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BULLOCK: Well, your Honor, this Court did hold in Berman and Midkiff that the police power and eminent domain power are coterminous. That was a holding especially of this Court's opinion in Midkiff. And there are certain amici that have been filed in this case, amicus briefs filed in this case that have called upon this Court to re-examine that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Does the record tell us anything about how often takings by eminent domain for economic development occur in this country? Is it frequent? What are we dealing with?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Bullock (respondent)",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It is, it is frequent, Your Honor. There's no -- we do not know of any study that looks specifically at condemnations for economic development, but after the Michigan court's decision in Poletown, they became commonplace."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Let me ask you this, and it's a little opposite of the particular question presented. Are there any writings or scholarship that indicates that when you have property being taken from one private person ultimately to go to another private person, that what we ought to do is to adjust the measure of compensation, so that the owner -- the condemnee -- can receive some sort of a premium for the development?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can I ask you about the standard. Go back for a second."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But do you do that area by area? I mean, one of the points you made, this is divided into what, seven areas?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Bullock (respondent)",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0001-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We believe it should be done where the property is actually being conveyed. And we think that that is the proper -- that is the proper test."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Horton (respondent)",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The principal purpose of the takings clause is to provide for just compensation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Horton",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, there are some plaintiffs who are not going to sell at any price. They want to stay there. You've got a severe assembly problem in this case and it's not as though you can say, well, go somewhere else. You have a situation where you've got the Pfizer plant that is being built there. You've got the state park that's there. You've got this Naval underseas facility that just came on the market. That's the only place anything is going to work and it's -- and it's five to six square miles of town. I mean, there is no other place to go."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "How much, how much of this was voluntarily sold, is that correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under Connecticut law, if the property owner is offered $100 but goes to the jury and gets $200, does the property owner have to pay the attorneys' fees or does the state pay the attorneys' fees?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 0.5,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "But the loans don't make him whole. Isn't --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2004-04-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "69e225fce6ed6d8ae631180d3c08ad821a753c2641ae86631ac5ffc14889a61d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-69e225fce6ed-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What is the remedy?"
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
